A||||9||LemonSky||price-gouging brake service||||8||
Z||000000||LemonSky||01-11-2001||11:00 PM||||Hi, everyone, I'm about to place my 86 SE6 on the market, and it's in really good shape, but just to make sure that no gremlins have surreptitiously sabotaged anything, I had a well-known auto shoppe perform a diagnostic test. They said the car only needed new belts (which I knew due to the slight squealing), and a brake line flush (which made some sense considering the slight amount of air in my brakes). So for both of these services, the cost would be $100.00. Sounded fine with me, so I took my car in for them to make the repairs. Several hours later, I get a phone call from them saying that the entire brake system would need overhauling and the cost would be $700.00! Now how could it go from a simple brake system flush to total brake overhaul? I told them to forget it!<P>Unfortunately, I'm an auto-repairing novice. Any advice on how to deal with my brakes? Like I said, there's just air in the line which, obviously,  makes it slightly difficult in stopping immediately. I've tried bleeding them before, but some air stubbornly manages to remain in there. By the way, there's no grinding or piercing sounds. Thanks.||||reg||8
Z||000001||2birds||01-11-2001||11:18 PM||||Tell them thanks for the estimate, I'd like a second opinion, and get one (or two).  Post their findings here and see what replies you get.<BR>I'd help you do it, but Atlanta is a bit far away.  Seems to me there are lots of members from Hotlanta here, though. Maybe somebody knows a reputable shop.<P>Get you in the door, then sell you more. That's how they make money.  Had an oil change joint try to sell my wife a replacement air filter for her t-bird once while I was at sea, and she pointed to the K&N sticker on the airbox and told him to get bent.  They lost any future business from me.||||reg||1
Z||000002||tgowens||01-11-2001||11:29 PM||||I think my question would be, how is the air getting into the lines?? Master cylinder maybe? bad caliper maybe? Possibly that is the reason for the increase in the estimate......sometimes it is hard to tell until you actually get a good look at the entire system.........||||reg||1
Z||000003||fierospeeder||01-12-2001||12:09 AM||||You can replace the entire brake system for half the cost or even more.<P><BR>master cylinder, calipers, rotors, pads, brake hoses.  Brake lines if you really want too.<P>Rebuilding a caliper isn't that hard, they have kits at the parts store.<P>I would find another mechanic, or do it myself.||||reg||1
Z||000004||2xsess||01-12-2001||02:42 AM||||Try emailing member Songman, if I remember correctly he knows a few shop owners near Atlanta, or maybe he can point you in the right direction.<P>How much are you thinking of asking for it?<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>AOL IM : Only2xsess<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/My_car_(large)sig_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||rogergarrison||01-12-2001||09:39 AM||||if i remember rite, you can buy a whole upgrade kit, calipers, rotors, pads and everything except the lines for around $500. And id pretty well guess thats lots more than youd need. Since you dont have any real problems, at most id guess you just might need a couple of front calipers, maybe rotors and a set of pads. Prob could do what it needs for less than $150.||||reg||1
Z||000006||2birds||01-12-2001||09:03 PM||||Last time I checked, front rebulit calipers were $30/ea, rears were around $65/ea, Bendix Semi-metallics were $20/axle.  As long as you return the core calipers, that's only $230, at your friendly Discount Auto.<BR>  Master cylinder and hoses would add a bit more, but I don't think it's that bad.  My set of Goodridge braided hoses at $80 cost $20 less than a set of stock ones.  <BR>Let's say maximum $400 in parts, not counting any markup.  Sounds like they're trying to sell you an entirely rebuilt system, plus about 4-6 hours of labor @ $50/hour.  A second opinion would get you an idea if you really need to replace everything, plus all the extra labor involved||||reg||1
Z||000007||Bob Englert||01-12-2001||09:07 PM||||AutoZone sells remanufactured rear calipers for the 84-87 Fieros foe $32 each plus exchange.  Best deal I have found on rear brakes.<P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
Z||000008||MrPBody||01-12-2001||09:24 PM||||Raydar or 1FST2M6 may know of a good local Fiero shop.  I usually do my own work, but haven't gotten into Fiero brakes yet.<P>I remember reading a few posts here about the difficulty of bleeding Fiero brakes due to the slanted master cylinder.  Try searching for info.  Beats paying $700.<P>Failing that, maybe other Georgia Fieros member can help.  We didn't meet in November or December, but we are meeting last Saturday of this month, I think.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||2birds||01-12-2001||10:41 PM||||Bob Englert,<BR>Thanks for the tip on the rear calipers.  <P>MrPBody,<BR>84-87's are simple.  Easier than drum brakes.<BR>Bleeding brakes and clutches is a lot easier with a $25 Mity-vac from Wally World.<p>[This message has been edited by 2birds (edited 01-12-2001).]||||reg||1
